<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    What leaves the barrel. Thin on purpose: the BALLISTICS live on the weapon
    (equipables.xml <fire> owns speed, spread, range, pellets, recoil), and this
    file owns what is true of the ammunition wherever it is fired from. Nine
    weapons share 9mm_bullet, so anything written here applies to all nine.

    <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        How fast this round travels, as a factor of the reference speed. The
        weapon's <fire speedMultiplier=> scales it: an arrow is slow whatever
        fires it, and a stronger bow makes that same arrow faster.

    <damage type="piercing|melee|explosion|energy"/>
        Which resistance applies to it when no weapon fired it (a frag's
        fragments). A round a weapon fires takes the weapon's own type.

    <lifetime durationMs="..."/>  <onDestroy><explosion .../></onDestroy>
        Grenade-shaped rounds: how long before it goes off, and the blast. For a
        cooked throwable (equipables.xml <throw>) the fuse starts at the pin.

    <bounce restitution="0.45" maxBounces="3"/>
        Fused rounds only. Glances off walls, doors, rocks and trees instead of
        stopping on them: each bounce mirrors the path about the surface and
        keeps `restitution` (0.05..1) of the speed and of the distance it had
        left. After maxBounces, or when too little is left, it stops where it
        hits, as it does without this. Players and ghouls always stop it.

    <fragments projectile="frag_fragment" count="22" range="400" damage="40"
               buildingDamage="40" knockback="0" speedMultiplier="1.0"
               spread="1" rangeVariation="0.6" speedVariation="0.4" falloff="0.4"/>
        Inside <onDestroy>: a frag's fragments. `count` (at most 48) REAL
        projectiles of the `projectile` entry below fly out of the blast, one
        somewhere in each slice of the circle (`spread`, 0..1, is how much of
        its slice it may wander over). Each falls up to `rangeVariation`
        (0..0.9) short of `range` (at most 2000) and flies up to
        `speedVariation` (0..0.9) slower than the fastest, so the burst is
        ragged rather than a ring. Each one flies, collides and is drawn
        exactly like a bullet: it stops at the first wall, rock, tree, player
        or ghoul in its way, which takes `damage` (the fragment's <damage
        type>, so armour counts) or, for buildings and resources,
        `buildingDamage` (the same as `damage` unless stated, like a gun's),
        with the same hit shake a gunshot gives them. `falloff` (0..1, default
        1 = none) is the share of both still done by a fragment landing at
        `range`, falling in a straight line from all of it at the blast, like
        a gun's <falloff minMultiplier>. Nothing protects the thrower from
        their own. Fewer fragments reach a far target too, and cover in front
        absorbs them.
        The fastest fly at the fragment entry's <physics baseSpeed> times
        speedMultiplier. A grenade lying against a wall loses the fragments
        heading into it; the rest fly out from its face. Every fragment is an
        entity sent to every watcher, which is why the count is capped.
        With or without an <explosion>.

    <cloud kind="gas" tiles="30" growMs="3000" holdMs="10000" fadeMs="3000"
           tickMs="500" damage="6" damageType="gas">
        <onHit condition="choking"/>
    </cloud>
        Inside <onDestroy>: something left lying on the ground. It spreads over
        at most `tiles` tiles (up to 255), nearest first, within `radius` tiles
        (default: what `tiles` covers as a disc plus one; at most 12). Walls
        and closed doors stop it; a room fills and a doorway lets it out. It
        grows over growMs, stays holdMs, thins out over fadeMs (each at most
        60000). Every tickMs while grown or holding, each player and ghoul
        standing in it takes `damage` of `damageType` (a wearable's
        <modifier key="gasResistance"> reduces "gas") and gets the <onHit>
        condition. kind is what clients draw: gas, smoke or fire.
        A gas or fire cloud landing on one of the same type that is still
        burning (same kind, damage, timings and condition) does not lie on top
        of it: it feeds it. The first cloud takes in the new one's tiles and
        starts its hold again, and the latest thrower gets the kills, so five
        molotovs on one spot are one fire: one damage tick, one cloud on the
        wire, one fire to draw. Smoke clouds always stay separate.
        buildingDamage, burns="wood,cloth": every tick, each building on or
        right next to a covered tile whose material is listed takes
        buildingDamage (fire burns a wooden wall, not a stone one). An object's
        material is its material= in objects.xml, or read off its
        <client particles=>: wood/woodlight wood, fur cloth, metal/steel/gold
        metal, stone stone.
        blocksVision (default true for kind="smoke", false otherwise): while
        it is there, until half faded, nobody is sent a player, ghoul or
        anything else that moves if the line to it crosses from clear air
        into smoke or back - so from outside you see neither into nor past
        it, and from inside you see only the inside. Ghouls cannot target
        through it either. Over the first half of the fade it gives its tiles
        back last-grown first, so it shrinks to where it went off rather than
        lifting everywhere at once.

    <shatter/>
        Breaks where it lands or on the first thing it hits (a molotov's
        bottle) and goes off there and then: never parks, never bounces. With a
        cookable <throw>, <lifetime> is only how long it can be held lit; one
        dropped or held too long breaks at the thrower's feet.

    <explosion ... throughWall="0"/>
        The share of damage, knockback and condition chance that reaches a
        creature with a wall, rock or tree between it and the blast. 0 (the
        default, on every explosion in every file) means cover protects
        completely; 1 means walls do not matter. Buildings are never sheltered.

    <onHit condition="toxic_poison" chance="0.5" durationMs="0" chancePerDamage="0"/>
        A CONDITION the round inflicts on a player it lands on (conditions.xml).
        durationMs overrides the condition's own first-stage length for THIS
        source (0 = use its own), so one `stun` can be brief from a dart and long
        from a grenade. chancePerDamage adds to the chance per point of damage
        that ACTUALLY landed, so a solid hit is likelier to land it than a graze
        - and armour, which cut the damage, cuts the chance with it.
        An <explosion> accepts the same element, for what the BLAST inflicts.
        chance is 0..1 and defaults to 1. It only fires on a hit that actually
        did damage, so a target protected by ghost mode or invincibility is not
        poisoned either.

        NOTHING SHIPPED USES THIS YET - the capability landed 2026-08-13 and
        which round should poison is a balance decision. A key that names no
        condition is reported at startup.
-->
<projectiles>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="0" key="9mm_bullet">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-bullet1"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-bullet2"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-bullet2l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="1" key="762_round">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-bullet3"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-bullet4"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-bullet4l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="2" key="12g_pellet">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-bullet5"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-bullet6"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-bullet6l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="3" key="wood_arrow">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-wood-arrow"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-wood-arrow1"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-wood-arrowl"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="4" key="spear">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-wood-spear0"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-wood-spear1"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-wood-spearl"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="5" key="wood_crossarrow">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-wood-crossarrow"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-wood-crossarrow1"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-wood-crossarrowl"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="6" key="nail">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-nail1"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-nail2"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-nail2l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <projectile clientProjectileId="7" key="energy">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="energy"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-laser0"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-laser1"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-laser1l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- Grenades draw with render="tumble": one sprite, the one in the hand
         (every frame names it), for the whole flight. It rolls with the
         ground it covers, `roll` world units per radian (default 24), so it
         spins off the hand, slows with each bounce and lies still where it
         stops; a streak of `trail` ("r,g,b", or "none") follows it while
         it is fast. No in-flight or landed frames to draw. -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="8" key="granade">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <lifetime durationMs="3000" />
        <bounce restitution="0.45" maxBounces="3"/>
        <onDestroy>
            <explosion area="1" playerDamage="200" buildingDamage="400" knockback="20">
                <onHit condition="slowed" chance="1" durationMs="600" strength="0.2"/>
            </explosion>
        </onDestroy>
        <client render="tumble">
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-hand-offensive-grenade"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-hand-offensive-grenade"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-hand-offensive-grenade"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- The frag: a small blast and a ring of fragments. Lethal in the open,
         survivable behind anything solid. The offensive grenade above is the
         opposite: a big blast and shove, nothing that flies. -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="9" key="frag_grenade">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <lifetime durationMs="3500" />
        <bounce restitution="0.45" maxBounces="3"/>
        <onDestroy>
            <explosion radius="90" playerDamage="40" buildingDamage="30" knockback="4"/>
            <fragments projectile="frag_fragment" count="22" range="400" damage="40" buildingDamage="40"
                       spread="1" rangeVariation="0.6" speedVariation="0.4" falloff="0.4"/>
        </onDestroy>
        <client render="tumble">
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-hand-frag-grenade"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-hand-frag-grenade"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-hand-frag-grenade"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- The gas grenade: no blast at all. A cloud that fills a room or a
         corridor and hurts whoever stays in it. -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="10" key="gas_grenade">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <lifetime durationMs="2500" />
        <bounce restitution="0.45" maxBounces="3"/>
        <onDestroy>
            <cloud kind="gas" tiles="30" growMs="3000" holdMs="10000" fadeMs="3000"
                   tickMs="500" damage="6" damageType="gas">
                <onHit condition="choking"/>
            </cloud>
        </onDestroy>
        <client render="tumble" trail="206,222,140">
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-hand-gas-grenade"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-hand-gas-grenade"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-hand-gas-grenade"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- The smoke grenade: no blast, no damage. A thick cloud nobody can see
         into, out of or through; the server stops sending what it hides. -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="11" key="smoke_grenade">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <lifetime durationMs="2000" />
        <bounce restitution="0.45" maxBounces="3"/>
        <onDestroy>
            <cloud kind="smoke" tiles="40" growMs="2000" holdMs="15000" fadeMs="4000"/>
        </onDestroy>
        <client render="tumble" trail="226,228,226">
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-hand-smoke-grenade"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-hand-smoke-grenade"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-hand-smoke-grenade"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- The molotov: lit like a grenade's pin is pulled, it breaks where it
         lands and leaves a patch of fire that burns whoever stands in it,
         keeps burning them a moment after they leave, and eats wooden
         buildings next to it. Stone and metal do not burn. -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="12" key="molotov">
        <physics baseSpeed="1.0"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <lifetime durationMs="8000" />
        <shatter/>
        <onDestroy>
            <cloud kind="fire" tiles="9" radius="2" growMs="500" holdMs="8000" fadeMs="1500"
                   tickMs="500" damage="8" damageType="fire" buildingDamage="90" burns="wood,cloth">
                <onHit condition="burning"/>
            </cloud>
        </onDestroy>
        <!-- render="molotov": the client draws the bottle (frame 0) tumbling
             `spin` radians per ms with its rag alight at (flameX, flameY) from
             the bottle's middle, trailing fire. Frames are kept for the shape
             every projectile entry has. -->
        <client render="molotov" flameX="24" flameY="-3" flameSize="22" spin="0.014">
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-hand-molotov"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-hand-molotov"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-hand-molotov"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

    <!-- What a frag grenade throws out: small, fast, and real. Fired by the
         blast (<fragments projectile="frag_fragment">), never by a weapon, so
         its damage and range live on the grenade and only how it flies and
         looks lives here. 3.5 x the reference speed is faster than any gun's
         round and still inside what the wire can state (2.55 units/ms). -->
    <projectile clientProjectileId="13" key="frag_fragment">
        <physics baseSpeed="3.5"/>
        <damage type="piercing"/>
        <client>
            <frame index="0" sprite="day-bullet5"/>
            <frame index="1" sprite="day-bullet6"/>
            <frame index="2" sprite="day-bullet6l"/>
        </client>
    </projectile>

</projectiles>